    "evidence_text": "We describe our system and innovations, as well as comparing our results to a Naive Bayes baseline.\n\nOur system outperforms a Naive Bayes Support Vector Machine (NBSVM) baseline, which is frequently considered a \"strong baseline\" for many Natural Language Processing (NLP) related tasks (see Wang et al  ).\n\nSystem Description\tWe modify the method of Universal Langage Model Fine-tuning for Text Classification (ULMFiT) presented in Howard and Ruder  .\n\nThe primary steps are: Below we will give more detail on each step and the parameters used to generate our system.\n\nTo our knowledge this is the first time that the BPE toenization has been used with ULMFiT in a competition model.\n\nFor the LM, we selected an ASGD Weight-Dropped Long\n\nShort Term Memory (AWD_LSTM, described in Merity et al.  ) model included in Fast.ai.\n\nWe sample 20 random seeds (see more in section SECREF26 ) to find the best initialization for our gradient descent search.\n\nFrom these samples, we select the best validation F1 metric or Mean Squared Error (MSE) for use in our test submission.\n\nWe selected our single best random seed for the test submission.",
